{
  "version": 275,
  "released": "2026-08-31",
  "changelog": "v275: EVENT IDENTITY — THE CONTAINMENT AUTHORITY FLAG CAN BE RAISED ON A VAULT THAT ALREADY RAN. A vault that ran `bind-episodes --apply` before its host had the drag-out gesture filed every containment at the DEFAULT authority, so 48 `part_of` records landed `origin: \"proposed\"`. Once drag was live and every one of those placements had been reviewed and approved, the sanctioned re-run — `bind-episodes --apply --containment-authority applied` — did NOTHING, and said so in the one way nobody could read: `containment_members: 0`, which is exactly the line a run prints when the rung found nothing at all.\n\nTWO LAYERS, NOT ONE. I3c's filter (v273) drops a containment row whose (telling, episode) pair already carries an active binding — right for a person's own `same`, and blind to the one case it could not tell apart: the rung's OWN record from an earlier run, filed under the same `identity_id` at the other authority. Had a row got past that filter, `file_event_identity`'s canonical-bytes create-or-keep would have kept the proposal anyway, because the two authorities mint the SAME id and `bindings_dir` sends both origins to the SAME directory. The fold then kept publishing all 48 as `proposed_links` — `GROUPING_ORIGINS` excludes `proposed` — so a container card counting only `containments` showed zero members over a vault that held twenty.\n\nTHE SENTENCE WAS ALREADY IN THE CODE. `episode_fold_contract.CONTAINMENT_AUTHORITY_RULE_TEXT` has promised since I2b that the flag \"chooses one field — `origin` — and that field is outside the binding digest, so flipping it re-keys nothing, rewrites no evidence and moves no file\". The honest completion of that sentence is an in-place move of the one field the flag owns: `proposed` -> `deterministic`, same file, same identity_id, same evidence, the ORIGINAL `created_at` preserved, counted as `containment_upgrades` rather than as a creation — because nothing was decided that was not decided before; only the host grew the gesture that lets a person take the placement back.\n\nTHE HALF THAT IS DELIBERATELY NOT SYMMETRIC. `deterministic` -> `proposed` is not a move. A run at `proposed` authority meeting a filed `deterministic` record KEEPS it and COUNTS the keep (`containment_kept_stronger`), because a host that forgot its flag must not silently un-draw a containment a person can already see and already drag out — and because a silent keep is indistinguishable from a rung that found nothing, which is the whole defect. A record differing in anything besides `origin` is not an upgrade target at all: ordinary create-or-keep stands and the difference is reported by name.\n\nPROVED ON THE BYTES, NOT ON THE PROMISE. `refile_event_identity` is a SECOND door, not a change to the first: audit A6's create-or-keep is what every other caller wants, and a writer that upgraded by default would make \"which origin is on disk\" depend on who ran last. Before it writes, it re-serializes the FILED record with its own origin and compares that to the file, so bytes an older normalization wrote are `kept_differs` rather than silently re-normalized under cover of an origin flip; and what it writes is the filed record with exactly ONE key replaced. `ORIGIN_UPGRADES` has one home and admits exactly one pair — every other pair of origins either says nothing changed or is a person's decision being overwritten by a rule.\n\nI3C'S INVARIANTS HOLD. The filter narrowed by exactly one case: a pair a person answered `same` on is still never re-placed, nothing under `sources/` moves, and the fold still loads clean after an upgrade — an origin move can never write a state `active_binding_index` refuses. The dry run and the weekly step both report what an apply WOULD move, so the owner-reviewed dry run the rollout gate requires can review the upgrade too rather than learn what moves by moving it.\n\nPROVEN, NOT ASSERTED. `test_event_identity_i2c_containment_authority_upgrade.py` files the record SHAPE a real vault carries, then proves the upgrade is in place (same file, same id, one field, the clock preserved), that the fold flips from `proposed_links` to `containments`, that a second applied run is a byte-identical no-op, that a run back at `proposed` keeps and counts rather than downgrades, and — the guard proven to FIRE — that with `ORIGIN_UPGRADES` emptied the same fixture reproduces the live defect exactly: nothing written, zero members reported, `proposed_links` still published.\n\nRehearsed end to end on a throwaway copy of a real vault brought to these bytes through `update.py --apply`: the upgrade run, the same run again as a no-op, `bind-episodes --dry-run` exit 0 clean, and `compile` exit 0 with the containments drawn.",
